<sn><md>### It matches exactly one character

Each dot consumes one character, no more and no less:

| Pattern | Matches | Doesn't match |
|---|---|---|
| `a.c` | abc, a1c, a c, a.c | ac, abbc |
| `a..c` | abbc, a12c | abc, ac |
| `...` | any three characters | "ab" |

To match a variable amount, combine it with a quantifier:

| Pattern | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `.*` | zero or more of any character |
| `.+` | one or more of any character |
| `.?` | zero or one of any character |
| `.{3}` | exactly three of any character |
| `.{2,5}` | between two and five of any character |
</md></sn><sn><md>### What it matches (and what it doesn't)

The dot matches letters, digits, spaces, tabs, punctuation, and symbols. Even a literal period matches, since a period is a character.

By default it does **not** match line breaks. In most engines that means `\n`, and some also exclude `\r`. So:

```
Pattern:  a.b
Text:     "a\nb"
Result:   no match
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Making the dot match newlines

Use the **dotall** (single-line) flag:

```python
# Python
re.search(r"a.b", "a\nb", re.DOTALL)   # matches
```

```javascript
// JavaScript
/a.b/s.test("a\nb")   // true
```

```
# Inline (PCRE, Java, .NET, Python)
(?s)a.b
```

Note the naming confusion: "single-line" mode (`s`) is about the dot, while "multiline" mode (`m`) is about `^` and `$`. They are unrelated.

If your engine lacks the flag, a common workaround is `[\s\S]`, which means "any whitespace or any non-whitespace," i.e. truly any character. (`[^]` works in JavaScript only.)
</md></sn><sn><md>### Greedy vs. lazy: the classic dot trap

`.*` is **greedy**: it grabs as much as it can, then backs off only if needed.

```
Pattern:  &lt;.*&gt;
Text:     "<b>bold</b> and <i>italic</i>"
Match:    "<b>bold</b> and <i>italic</i>"   (the whole thing)
```

It matched from the first `&lt;` to the very last `&gt;`. Often you want the shortest match instead. Add `?` to make it **lazy**:

```
Pattern:  &lt;.*?&gt;
Matches:  "<b>", "</b>", "<i>", "</i>"
```

Another option is to be more specific: `&lt;[^&gt;]*&gt;` means "a `&lt;`, then anything that isn't `&gt;`, then `&gt;`." This is usually faster and more predictable than a lazy dot.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Matching a literal period

Because `.` is a wildcard, a real period needs escaping:

| Pattern | Text "3.14 and 3x14" |
|---|---|
| `3.14` | matches both |
| `3\.14` | matches only "3.14" |

Inside a character class, the dot is already literal, so `[.]` also matches only a period.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Common pitfalls

1. **Forgetting to escape it.** `example.com` also matches "exampleXcom". Use `example\.com`.
2. **Overusing `.*`.** It can swallow far more than intended, especially across a long line. Prefer a specific character class when you know what should be there.
3. **Expecting it to cross lines.** A pattern like `start.*end` won't match if "start" and "end" are on different lines unless you enable dotall.
4. **Performance.** Multiple greedy dots, such as `.*foo.*bar.*`, can cause heavy backtracking on long input that fails to match.
5. **Unicode.** The dot generally matches one *code point*, not one visible character. An emoji or a letter with a combining accent may count as two or more dots in some engines.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Quick practice

Try these in regex101:

- `b.g` against "bag big bug bg b g". Which match? (bag, big, bug, "b g")
- `a.*z` against "a1z2z" matches how much? (All of it, because it's greedy.)
- `a.*?z` against the same text? (Just "a1z".)
- `file.txt` vs `file\.txt` against "file.txt fileXtxt". How many matches for each? (Two vs. one.)
</md></sn>